[stderr] # 03 — Technical, data, trust, and quality contract
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Inputs:** [`INITIAL.md`](../INITIAL.md), [product brief](01-product-brief.md),
[stderr] and [feature model](02-feature-model.md). This contract makes the P0 offline
[stderr] single-player release implementable without selecting a vendor or creating a
[stderr] dependency on the contradictory connected-service requests. It is a design
[stderr] contract, not permission to collect data or operate services.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Architecture and requirement traceability
[stderr] 
[stderr] The production architecture is a static, client-side 2D web game: a shared
[stderr] deterministic domain core, browser presentation/input adapters, versioned
[stderr] authored content, and local durable storage. The web host may deliver a signed
[stderr] release bundle and service worker, but it is not a campaign authority. The
[stderr] core must have no browser APIs, timers, network calls, or renderer dependencies.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Component / boundary | Requirement served | Contract |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Game shell and responsive renderer | J1, J3, J6; readable mobile-to-desktop play | Owns navigation, focus/visibility pause, semantic UI, visual settings, and rendering only. It requests domain commands and renders returned state/events. |
[stderr] | Input adapters | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad accessibility | Convert platform events into a shared, remappable `InputAction` vocabulary; unavailable hardware is non-fatal. No adapter may mutate campaign state directly. |
[stderr] | Deterministic rules core | Tutorial combat, quests, inventory, progression | Owns legal state transitions, seeded/randomness policy, combat resolution, reward ledger, and invariants. Command validation precedes every mutation. |
[stderr] | Content registry and validator | One town/dungeon, original content, localisation | Loads the active versioned content package; resolves IDs/keys and rejects invalid packages before play. It is read-only at runtime. |
[stderr] | Save/recovery repository | J5, long-session recovery | Owns atomic local snapshot writes, validation, quarantine, migration, retention, export/import when approved, and explicit deletion. |
[stderr] | PWA release manager | J7, install/offline/update safety | Owns precache manifest, cache integrity, install affordance, and safe-boundary activation. It never replaces a running release or a pending save write. |
[stderr] | Error boundary and consent-gated diagnostics | Crash/error recovery and privacy | Shows retry/title/recovery actions. Local diagnostic records are minimal and removable; no telemetry transport exists in P0. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Dependency direction and state rules
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```text
[stderr] authored content ──> content validator ──> deterministic rules core <── input actions
[stderr]                                             │
[stderr] web/PWA shell + renderer <── state/events ─┼──> save/recovery repository
[stderr]         │                                   │
[stderr]         └──────────> PWA release manager ──┴──> error boundary / local diagnostics
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] Only the rules core can produce durable campaign state. The shell owns
[stderr] ephemeral UI state (open panels, selection, animation), while the repository
[stderr] owns only serialized snapshots and recovery metadata. Rendering, input,
[stderr] caching, and diagnostics must not be required to make a legal game decision.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Data contract
[stderr] 
[stderr] ### Ownership, lifecycle, and retention
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Persistence, migration, and failure semantics
[stderr] 
[stderr] Save commit is a write-ahead replacement protocol: serialize a complete
[stderr] snapshot → validate checksum/schema/invariants → write candidate under a new
[stderr] revision → verify read-back → mark current → retain prior known-good snapshot.
[stderr] Implementations must use browser storage semantics that make the final pointer
[stderr] switch atomic, or emulate it with immutable revision records and a validated
[stderr] current pointer. Never clear the prior save before the new pointer is valid.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Condition | Required behaviour |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Storage quota, permission, or unavailable local storage | Keep session playable; show non-blocking save failure and retry guidance; do not claim persistence succeeded. |
[stderr] | Interrupted/crashed write | Ignore incomplete candidate, retain/reload prior confirmed snapshot, and show recovery state at next launch. |
[stderr] | Malformed/checksum-invalid save | Quarantine bytes and metadata; do not overwrite; offer last valid checkpoint/new game and support/export guidance where feasible. |
[stderr] | Migration failure or unsupported version | Preserve original, fail closed into quarantine, and offer clear version/fallback choices. |
[stderr] | Bad content package/reference | Fail build/CI; if discovered at runtime, stop the affected route, retain save, and offer title/retry rather than corrupting campaign state. |
[stderr] | Cache/network refresh failure | Continue with known-good cached release; explain first-install offline limitation without pretending an uncached release is playable. |
[stderr] | Application fault/crash loop | Error boundary offers retry, title, and safe recovery; diagnostics remain local unless explicitly consented and a future transport is approved. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] Migration functions are ordered, pure transformations from one explicit schema
[stderr] version to the next, with fixtures for every supported predecessor. They must
[stderr] be idempotent or record the source version so a retry cannot apply a migration
[stderr] twice. A release may remove old migrations only after a separately approved
[stderr] support/retention decision.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Trust, privacy, and security
[stderr] 
[stderr] - Treat content, imported saves, URL parameters, browser events, service-worker
[stderr]   messages, and persisted bytes as untrusted. Validate and bound sizes before
[stderr]   parsing; never execute content as code or render dialogue via unsafe HTML.
[stderr] - Serve production assets over HTTPS with a restrictive content security policy,
[stderr]   no third-party scripts by default, dependency lock/review, and build-time
[stderr]   asset integrity/hash manifest. Do not embed credentials in the client.
[stderr] - Use same-origin storage isolation; minimise persisted data to game state and
[stderr]   chosen preferences. Do not collect names, contact data, precise location,
[stderr]   advertising IDs, account identifiers, payment data, or gameplay analytics in
[stderr]   P0.
[stderr] - Diagnostics default off where consent is required and must state fields,
[stderr]   purpose, retention, deletion, and transport before collection. A local
[stderr]   diagnostic record excludes free-text dialogue and complete save contents.
[stderr] - There is no authorization role in P0 because there is no server resource or
[stderr]   identity boundary. Locally, a player controls their browser profile; this is
[stderr]   not a security guarantee against a device owner. Any server feature requires
[stderr]   explicit least-privilege roles, authenticated authorization on every request,
[stderr]   rate limits, audit events, deletion/retention policy, incident ownership, and
[stderr]   documented offline/outage behaviour before implementation.
[stderr] - No copied branding, assets, text, layouts, or distinctive interaction
[stderr]   sequences enter the content pipeline. Provenance/review is required for
[stderr]   shipped art, audio, writing, and dependencies.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Decisions deferred for approval
[stderr] 
[stderr] These are intentionally not selected by this contract: game engine/framework,
[stderr] rendering library, static host/CDN, CI provider, diagnostics vendor/transport,
[stderr] exact storage API/quota, browser/OS/device minimums, performance thresholds,
[stderr] languages, age/parental/regional obligations, service-worker rollout policy,
[stderr] and content editor format. Account identity, remote save replication,
[stderr] multiplayer/player safety, live operations, analytics, purchases, and
[stderr] entitlements require separate product, security, privacy, cost, failure-mode,
[stderr] and support approval before they are allowed to alter this architecture.
[stderr] # Product brief — Mock Fantasy Quest 4
[stderr] 
[stderr] **Source of truth:** [`INITIAL.md`](INITIAL.md). This normalized brief supports
[stderr] downstream planning; it preserves source scope and records unknowns rather than
[stderr] choosing them.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Product problem and outcome
[stderr] 
[stderr] Casual adult players need a compact, approachable fantasy RPG that delivers a
[stderr] complete story without requiring a long commitment or dependable connection.
[stderr] The first useful session is successful when a new player finishes a first
[stderr] turn-based battle and understands how to save and resume. The release outcome
[stderr] is an original short campaign that a player can complete through exploration,
[stderr] dialogue, quests, combat, rewards, saving, and a short ending.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Category, users, and core experience
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Area | Normalized first-release statement |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Category | Deliberately small, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG. |
[stderr] | Users | Casual and adult players, including people using keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad, captions, adjustable text, and visual settings. |
[stderr] | Campaign boundary | One town, one dungeon, and one guided, short ending; no further regions or chapters are required. |
[stderr] | Core loop | Explore, converse, accept or advance a quest, enter party-based turn-based combat, manage rewards/inventory, save, and continue. |
[stderr] | Experience direction | Original warm hand-painted fantasy, readable silhouettes, and a restrained palette. |
[stderr] | Functional reference | *Final Fantasy V* may inform only the high-level scope of a party-based turn-based adventure. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Selected target systems
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Target | First-release role | Input model | Delivery constraints | Out-of-scope boundary |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Responsive public web application | Primary browser surface for the complete game. | Keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad. | Responsive phone/tablet/desktop layout; browser compatibility and performance budget must be approved before implementation. | Native binaries, a separate web-only campaign, and browser-exclusive mechanics. |
[stderr] | Progressive web app (PWA) | Installable delivery of the same shared game core. | Same inputs where the browser/device supports them. | Offline assets and save-data behavior; safe update/cache refresh must preserve confirmed player data. | A PWA-exclusive campaign, mandatory continuous connection, or a different ruleset. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] The product uses a shared core with platform adaptation only where necessary.
[stderr] It should work on widely used devices and under feasible slow-network
[stderr] conditions; exact supported browsers, device classes, storage quotas, and
[stderr] network floor are open decisions.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## First-release scope and measurable success measures
[stderr] 
[stderr] Included scope: playable exploration; one town and one dungeon; narrative,
[stderr] dialogue, quests, progression and a short ending; onboarding/tutorial; at least
[stderr] one tutorialized turn-based battle; inventory and rewards; save/load, resume,
[stderr] checkpoints, data-loss recovery; settings and accessibility; audio/visual
[stderr] feedback; content/level delivery; and crash reporting with player-facing error
[stderr] recovery. The source also names account/identity, multiplayer/player safety,
[stderr] live-service operations, and purchase/entitlement handling, but their release
[stderr] status remains contingent on the explicit conflict decision below.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The release is measurable when:
[stderr] 
[stderr] - A new player can finish the tutorial battle, create a save, reload it, and
[stderr]   reach the short ending on each supported target.
[stderr] - The delivered campaign contains exactly one town, one dungeon, and one short
[stderr]   ending; added regions or chapters are not needed for release completion.
[stderr] - Both targets meet their stated input role; the PWA is installable and each
[stderr]   target has a tested offline, save, update, and error-recovery path.
[stderr] - Dialogue, inventory, quest, and progression UI are readable with approved
[stderr]   accessibility settings, and captions/visual settings are available where the
[stderr]   corresponding feedback exists.
[stderr] - An interruption, recoverable error, or safe update offers a player-facing
[stderr]   recovery path and does not silently discard the last confirmed save.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Later roadmap, non-goals, and reference boundary
[stderr] 
[stderr] Deferred roadmap: live events/seasonal content, player-created/shared content
[stderr] (levels, mods, designs, stories), advanced analytics, and advanced
[stderr] monetisation. They are excluded from first-release acceptance.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Non-goals: copied branding, assets, text, layouts, characters, story/lore,
[stderr] audio, maps, or distinctive interactions; additional towns/dungeons/campaigns;
[stderr] native apps; and platform-specific game variants. No cloud-sync, payment,
[stderr] moderation, service-uptime, or backend commitment is implied before approval.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Reference boundary | Prohibited | Permitted |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | Branding and copy | Names, logos, trademarks, marketing copy, story text, dialogue, characters, and world/lore. | A high-level description of a compact party-based turn-based adventure. |
[stderr] | Assets and presentation | Art, music, sound, maps, screen composition, visual language, and layouts. | Original art and UI that fulfil this brief’s own direction. |
[stderr] | Interaction design | Reproducing distinctive combat screens, menus, flows, quests, or interaction choreography. | Independently designed mechanics and feedback appropriate to the requested scope. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Constraints and safe assumptions
[stderr] 
[stderr] Explicit constraints: web/PWA delivery, offline play, accessibility,
[stderr] localisation, privacy, safe save/recovery, slow-network feasibility, and
[stderr] original expression. Safe working assumptions: the offline core does not block
[stderr] on a remote service; content/UI are original; local persistence is durable
[stderr] enough for confirmed saves where browser storage permits; connected features,
[stderr] if approved, degrade visibly; and crash diagnostics minimize personal data.
[stderr] These are guardrails, not approval of a stack, vendor, backend, account model,
[stderr] data-retention policy, or technical promise.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Open decisions requiring human approval
[stderr] 
[stderr] 1. **Offline versus connected scope:** Reconcile the offline single-player
[stderr]    premise with required account identity, multiplayer safety, live operations,
[stderr]    and purchase/entitlement handling. Classify each as release-critical,
[stderr]    optional connected enhancement, or deferred.
[stderr] 2. **Account and data policy:** Decide whether accounts are absent, optional,
[stderr]    or mandatory; then define authentication, sync/conflict behavior, retention,
[stderr]    deletion, and offline/service-outage recovery.
[stderr] # Factory collection
[stderr] 
[stderr] This directory contains the runbook-generator's source collection. Read the
[stderr] repository [README](../README.md) for the end-to-end workflow and Supervisor
[stderr] configuration.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Source files and roles
[stderr] 
[stderr] | File | Purpose |
[stderr] | --- | --- |
[stderr] | [F001.md](F001.md)–[F016.md](F016.md) | **F-series factory runbooks.** They turn the brief into a generated project's detailed plan, complete work checklist, and B-series runbook-writing files. |
[stderr] | [TEMPLATE.md](TEMPLATE.md) | Required structure for a normal manually written runbook: goal, steps, success checks, and evidence. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] This directory contains only the reusable factory source. It never contains the
[stderr] thousands of runbooks for a particular product.
[stderr] 
[stderr] | Series | Plain-English purpose | Output |
[stderr] | --- | --- | --- |
[stderr] | F-series | The factory: understand the brief, identify the required domains, and design a complete delivery plan. | A workspace in `projects/<slug>/`, including its specification, work checklist, and B-series files. |
[stderr] | B-series | The runbook writers: write the next small set of implementation instructions. “Bounded” means one B file may write no more than seven R files. | Up to seven R-series files per B task; extra B files are created when more areas need coverage. |
[stderr] | C-series | Catalogue checkpoints: validate the authoring catalogue, allocations, dependencies, and limits before the next writing wave. | No product work. C files are internal coordination files in `projects/<slug>/authoring-runbooks/`. |
[stderr] | D-series | Dispatchers: expand one eligible planning chapter and create the next bounded B-series writing wave when more work remains. | More B-series authoring files, not R files or product work. D files live beside B/C files in `projects/<slug>/authoring-runbooks/`. |
[stderr] | R-series | The product-work instructions: describe one small, ordered piece of real work. | Source changes, tests, assets, documentation, or other deliverables required by that R file. |
[stderr] 
[stderr] `C` means **Catalogue checkpoint** and `D` means **Dispatcher**. They are
[stderr] factory-internal coordination runbooks, so they are far less important to the
[stderr] application than the F/B/R chain: they neither define the product nor build it.
[stderr] They exist only to let a very large catalogue grow safely without duplicating,
[stderr] skipping, or overloading B-series writers. They share `authoring-runbooks/` and
[stderr] its durable state with B files because all three operate in the runbook-writing
[stderr] layer, before R-series product work is run.
[stderr] 
[stderr] The generated project's `planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json` also
[stderr] contains `IMP-*` **implementation catalogue records**. These are stable
[stderr] planning/traceability entries, not runbooks and not another name for R files.
[stderr] The normal chain is `REQ/NFR requirement → specification → IMP record → B
[stderr] writer → R runbook`. `REQ-*` means a functional requirement (a behaviour or
[stderr] capability); `NFR-*` means a non-functional requirement (a quality or
[stderr] constraint such as accessibility, security, performance, or privacy). They
[stderr] live in `specification/requirements.md`, `requirements.json`, and the
[stderr] `traceability-matrix.md` for the generated project. An IMP record may map to
[stderr] one or several R files, or wait for a dependency, so `REQ-0007`, `NFR-0004`,
[stderr] `IMP-0007`, and `R0007` intentionally have independent IDs. Each generated R
[stderr] file records the relevant requirement IDs in `requirement_ids` and the relevant
[stderr] IMP IDs in `source_catalogue_ids` front matter.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every R-series file must make an explicit asset decision in its front matter:
[stderr] `asset_impact: required` with stable comma-separated `asset_ids`, or
[stderr] `asset_impact: not_applicable` with a blank `asset_ids`. Asset-required files
[stderr] also describe asset purpose, variants, ownership, original-art constraints, and
[stderr] verification in an `## Asset assessment` section. This lets the Supervisor use
[stderr] its opt-in asset lane only where the product work genuinely needs it.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every R-series file also makes an explicit audio decision: `audio_impact:
[stderr] required` with stable `audio_ids`, a cue brief, duration, loop policy, and
[stderr] audio-style version; or `audio_impact: not_applicable` with the documented
[stderr] empty/default values. When required, Supervisor uses the independent local
[stderr] audio lane—cue director, ACE-Step 1.5 XL Turbo generator, then technical and
[stderr] provenance QA—before the coding stage. F004 creates an audio-direction and cue
[stderr] map only when the product actually needs music, sound effects, voice, or
[stderr] audible feedback.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Every R-series file must also carry traceability and provenance metadata:
[stderr] `requirement_ids` identifies the `REQ-*` and `NFR-*` requirements it fulfils or
[stderr] verifies; `source_specifications` links to the smallest relevant canonical specification sections,
[stderr] `source_catalogue_ids` identifies its implementation-catalogue records,
[stderr] `authoring_batch` names its B-series writer, and `factory_stages` records the
[stderr] F-series stages that established the requirement. This lets an implementation
[stderr] agent retrieve focused canonical context without treating procedural F/B files
[stderr] as its requirements source.
[stderr] 
[stderr] For example, a game may eventually have R runbooks for story, combat, content,
[stderr] saves, and platform delivery; an IDE may have R runbooks for language services,
[stderr] editing, debugging, and extensions. The F-series derives those areas from the
[stderr] brief rather than assuming either example.
[stderr] 
[stderr] `F001`–`F004` understand the brief and domains, `F005`–`F010` make the delivery
[stderr] map, `F011`–`F013` create and check the specification and work checklist, and
[stderr] `F014` creates the first B-series runbook-writing files. When Supervisor runs
[stderr] those B files, each one writes up to seven R-series product-work runbooks. If
[stderr] more R files are required, B dispatcher files create more B files. `F015`–`F016`
[stderr] establish quality and handoff material.
[stderr] 
[stderr] ## Run the collection
[stderr] 
[stderr] From the repository root, create a named project brief with `supervisor initial`,
[stderr] review `projects/<project-name>/INITIAL.md`, then run:
[stderr] 
[stderr] ```zsh
[stderr] supervisor-run --project <project-name>
[stderr] ```
[stderr] 
[stderr] `--run-initial` runs only `F001`, which is useful for checking that the named
[stderr] brief can be normalized before committing to the full factory. `--project`
[stderr] reads that workspace's `INITIAL.md`, runs every F-series factory stage, then
[stderr] dynamically follows its registered B-series and R-series collections. It keeps
[stderr] state inside the named workspace and stops only for a real failure or explicit
[stderr] review gate.
[stderr] 
[stderr] Generated runbook state is kept in the named workspace's `.state/` directory,
