 to earlier shards.\"}\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:9:## Architecture and requirement traceability\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:27:### Dependency direction and state rules\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:42:## Data contract\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:44:### Ownership, lifecycle, and retention\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:60:### Versioned content and save schemas\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:99:## Commands, validation, idempotency, and concurrency\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:124:## Persistence, migration, and failure semantics\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:149:## Trust, privacy, and security\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:174:## Target-platform matrix\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:191:## Observability, quality evidence, and release verification\nprojects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md:212:## Decisions deferred for approval\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,150p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0006.md; sed -n '1,135p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0008.md; sed -n '1,130p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/runbooks/R0011.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n---\ntask_id: R0006\nsequence: 6\ntitle: Specify local save and recovery protocol\ndependencies: R0002,R0003,R0004\nrequirement_ids: REQ-0006,REQ-0012,NFR-0002,NFR-0003,NFR-0005\nsource_specifications: specification/03-technical-contract.md#persistence-migration-and-failure-semantics,specification/04-experience-contract.md#empty-error-recovery-destructive-and-privacy-sensitive-states\nsource_catalogue_ids: IMP-0006\nauthoring_batch: B0004\nfactory_stages: F011,F012,F013\nasset_impact: not_applicable\nasset_ids:\nasset_brief: No assets; this is a local data and recovery protocol.\naudio_impact: not_applicable\naudio_ids:\naudio_brief:\naudio_duration_seconds: 0\naudio_loop: not_applicable\naudio_style_version:\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nSpecify the bounded, local save and recovery protocol for immutable revisions,\nchecksums, validated current pointers, known-good retention, quarantine,\nseparate preferences, explicit deletion, and save-safe boundaries. This is a\nprotocol and fixture contract, not approval to select a browser-storage API or\npromise a quota, retention period, or migration-support window.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nR0002 provides the core/adapter seam, R0003 provides deterministic clock and\npersistence fixtures, and R0004 supplies the immutable versioned campaign\nsnapshot and command/revision contracts. Retain GATE-0003 / DEC-0003: storage\nAPI, quota, support window, and recovery policy require human approval before\nimplementation selects or relies on them. The gate blocks implementation\nchoices, not this contract's authoring.\n\n## Ownership\n\n- `src/core/save/{schema,compatibility,migrations}/` protocol contracts.\n- `src/platform/web/persistence/` adapter boundary.\n- `tests/{core,platform}/save*` fault and recovery fixtures.\n- `docs/save-and-recovery.md`.\n- Snapshot/preference/quarantine schemas and snapshot-repository/recovery API.\n\nThe shared layer owns snapshot shape, validation, compatibility, and recovery\nsemantics. The web adapter owns only the eventual approved local-storage\nmechanism. PWA cache/install lifecycle, content registry, remote sync,\nidentity, diagnostics transport, and campaign transitions are not owned here.\n\n## Required behaviour\n\nUse a write-ahead replacement protocol: serialize a complete immutable\nsnapshot, validate checksum/schema/invariants, write a new revision candidate,\nverify read-back, atomically mark a validated current pointer (or emulate it\nwith immutable records), and retain the prior known-good snapshot. Never clear\nor overwrite the prior confirmed save until the new pointer is valid. Separate\npreferences from campaign snapshots and never treat a cache record as a save.\n\nValidate bounded, untrusted persisted bytes before parsing or use. A malformed,\nchecksum-invalid, incompatible, or migration-failed candidate is preserved with\nits metadata in quarantine; it never replaces a valid save. Recovery offers\nthe last valid checkpoint or a clear new-game/fallback route. Loading restores\nonly safe scene boundaries. A failed quota, permission, or unavailable-storage\nattempt leaves the session playable, says persistence did not succeed, and\noffers non-blocking retry guidance.\n\nPersist only at explicit safe boundaries: no unresolved combat turn or partial\ncommand can become current. A stale writer must fail rather than replace the\nlatest revision. Deletion requires an explicit confirmation naming the selected\nslot and its available progress/time; initial focus is Cancel, repeated/stale\ninput cannot activate Delete, and only that local slot and its recovery\nmetadata are removed.\n\nMigration transformations remain ordered pure candidate transformations and\nare not implemented or approved here beyond their compatibility boundary; R0007\nowns the migration runner. No cloud, account, sync, cross-device merge, cache\neviction, or export/import promise is introduced.\n\n## Web and PWA differences\n\nWeb and installed PWA share the same local snapshot, preference, validation,\nquarantine, and recovery contract. The web adapter eventually binds an approved\nsame-origin storage mechanism; the PWA inherits it and must keep its\nservice-worker cache separate. A cache refresh or update failure neither\ndeletes, refreshes, nor synchronises save data, and no implementation may\nclaim persistence after an unconfirmed write.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nDo not choose a storage API, quota, eviction guarantee, support window,\nretention policy, or migration set before GATE-0003 / DEC-0003 approval. Do not\nimplement cloud sync, accounts, remote deletion, cross-device conflict\nresolution, PWA cache lifecycle, release update flow, diagnostics transport,\ncontent validation, gameplay, UI screens, product assets, or audio.\n\n## Asset assessment\n\n`asset_impact` is `not_applicable`: local snapshots, protocol interfaces, and\nfault fixtures require no product asset. Do not create or reserve incidental\nvisual, source, audio, icon, illustration, font, texture, sprite, map, or other\nproduct asset; `asset_ids` and audio IDs remain blank.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Run deterministic fault fixtures for interrupted write, failed read-back,\n  checksum failure, malformed/oversized bytes, unsupported version, storage\n  quota/permission/unavailable failures, stale writer, quarantine, retry, and\n  selected-slot deletion confirmation.\n- Prove each failure preserves the last valid snapshot, does not report a save\n  as successful, keeps the session usable where possible, and exposes the\n  required retry/title/fallback/new-game recovery choice.\n- Prove a current-pointer promotion occurs only after candidate validation and\n  read-back; prove preferences and PWA cache fixtures cannot be read as or\n  overwrite a campaign snapshot.\n- Prove a pending command or unresolved combat turn cannot be committed and a\n  stale/repeated delete action cannot remove an unselected slot.\n- Before recording the B0004 checkpoint, validate the runbook loader parses\n  this front matter, provenance is non-empty and maps to IMP-0006, dependencies\n  match the manifest, owned paths do not overlap R0005's content boundary, and\n  every required section is present. Stop on any mismatch; never overwrite an R contract.\n\n## Evidence\n\nRetain deterministic fault-fixture output, pre/post candidate and pointer\nrecords, quarantine and recovery-path assertions, preference/save/cache\nseparation evidence, deletion-confirmation assertions, the retained\nGATE-0003 / DEC-0003 record, and the pre-checkpoint loader, provenance,\ndependency, ownership, and required-section validation record.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Interrupted, invalid, incompatible, or failed save candidates cannot replace\n  a validated current snapshot; the prior known-good snapshot is retained or\n  malformed bytes are quarantined.\n- Save snapshots, preferences, and PWA cache records remain separate, and\n  local recovery presents clear retry, title, fallback, or new-game choices\n  without claiming an unconfirmed save succeeded.\n- Storage selection, quota, support window, migration policy, and recovery\n  policy remain blocked by GATE-0003 / DEC-0003; no remote, cache, or asset\n  work is introduced.\n---\ntask_id: R0008\nsequence: 8\ntitle: Bound P0 identity, authorization, and connected-service exclusions\ndependencies: R0002,R0004\nrequirement_ids: REQ-0013,REQ-0014,REQ-0015,NFR-0012\nsource_specifications: specification/03-technical-contract.md#trust-privacy-and-security\nsource_catalogue_ids: IMP-0008\nauthoring_batch: B0003\nfactory_stages: F011,F012,F013\nasset_impact: not_applicable\nasset_ids:\nasset_brief: No assets; a negative scope boundary.\naudio_impact: not_applicable\naudio_ids:\naudio_brief:\naudio_duration_seconds: 0\naudio_loop: not_applicable\naudio_style_version:\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nDocument and test the P0 negative boundary: local campaign start, save, and\nresume require no account identifier, token, remote profile, authorization\nrole, entitlement, multiplayer, moderation, safety, or connected service.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nR0002 supplies the core/adapter dependency seam and R0004 supplies the local\ncampaign state envelope. They are implementation dependencies. Retain\nGATE-0006 / DEC-0006: no connected feature may be implemented until its scope,\nprivacy, security, operational, cost, and failure-mode decisions are approved.\n\n## Ownership\n\n- `docs/security-boundary.md`.\n- `tests/core/connected-scope-exclusion.*`.\n- The future connected-service approval checklist and local-slot identity\n  contract.\n\n## Required behaviour\n\nState that the local browser profile is the P0 player boundary, not an account\nor authorization guarantee. The local campaign is the source of truth and has\nno remote profile, analytics stream, purchase ledger, match state, cloud\nbackup, role, privilege, or entitlement. Local slot selection is an opaque\nlocal reference only and must not contain identity, token, privilege, or remote\nprofile fields.\n\nAdd negative contract tests and a static boundary scan for P0 foundation\nmodules. They must fail on identity/token/role/entitlement fields, network or\nserver clients, sign-in/OAuth/sync paths, multiplayer/safety/moderation APIs,\npurchase flows, or remote campaign authority. Treat future replication, if\never approved, as a separate optional mirror rather than campaign truth.\n\n## Web and PWA differences\n\nResponsive web and installed PWA use the same local-slot contract and same\nconnected-scope exclusion. PWA installation, cache, and browser-origin storage\ndo not imply identity, authorization, sync, or a remote service. No target\nobtains a distinct connected implementation path.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nDo not implement sign-in, OAuth, accounts, profiles, tokens, roles,\nauthorization, synchronization, server APIs, analytics transport, multiplayer,\nmoderation, reporting, purchases, entitlements, cloud saves, remote\ndiagnostics, or security claims about a device owner. Do not create product\nassets, audio, or connected-service configuration.\n\n## Asset assessment\n\n`asset_impact` is `not_applicable`: this is documentation and negative test\ncoverage for an architectural exclusion. Do not create or reserve incidental\nvisual, source, audio, icon, illustration, font, texture, sprite, map, or\nother product assets; `asset_ids` remains blank.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Run a static scan across P0 foundation modules for prohibited identity,\n  token, privilege, entitlement, and network/server-client fields or imports.\n- Run negative fixtures that attempt prohibited connected-scope configuration\n  and prove the contract rejects or does not expose it.\n- Verify start, local save, and local resume fixtures require only a local slot\n  reference and no remote request or account data.\n- Review `docs/security-boundary.md` against the exclusion list and record\n  GATE-0006 / DEC-0006 as retained for any future connected proposal.\n\n## Evidence\n\nRetain static-scan and negative-fixture results, local start/save/resume test\noutput showing no remote request, the security-boundary review, and the future\nconnected-service approval checklist with its retained gate.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- P0 start, save, and resume expose no identity, token, role, entitlement,\n  remote-profile, or network-client field or dependency.\n- Negative tests prevent connected-service scope from entering P0 foundation\n  modules for both web and PWA delivery paths.\n- Any future connected work remains outside campaign truth and blocked by\n  GATE-0006 / DEC-0006 rather than implicitly approved.\n---\ntask_id: R0011\nsequence: 11\ntitle: Create original-content provenance control\ndependencies: R0005\nrequirement_ids: REQ-0001,REQ-0008,NFR-0008\nsource_specifications: specification/01-product-brief.md#originality-boundary,specification/04-experience-contract.md#experience-premise-and-originality-boundary\nsource_catalogue_ids: IMP-0011\nauthoring_batch: B0005\nfactory_stages: F011,F012,F013\nasset_impact: not_applicable\nasset_ids:\nasset_brief: No assets are created; this contract defines provenance controls for later asset-required work.\naudio_impact: not_applicable\naudio_ids:\naudio_brief:\naudio_duration_seconds: 0\naudio_loop: not_applicable\naudio_style_version:\nbrowser_impact: not_applicable\nplaywright_spec:\n---\n\n## Objective\n\nDefine the original-content provenance control that makes each later authored\ncontent package auditable before it enters a build. The control supplies a\nprovenance-record format, source-asset manifest input, validator contract, and\nhuman creative-review disposition without creating art, audio, story, or legal\napproval.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nR0005 provides the versioned content package, asset/provenance-manifest schema,\nand validator boundary. It is the implementation dependency. Human creative\nreview is mandatory for every authored package; legal or licensing review,\nwhere applicable, remains under GATE-0007 and cannot be inferred or automated.\n\n## Ownership\n\n- `assets/source-manifest.*` provenance input format for later authored\n  content/assets.\n- `docs/original-content-review.md` review checklist and recorded disposition.\n- `tests/content/provenance.*` missing-field and review-state fixtures.\n- Provenance manifest, review disposition data, and provenance-validator input\n  contract.\n\nR0005 retains content-schema and registry ownership. This task owns only the\nevidence required to admit later authored material; it does not own assets,\nlicences, gameplay content, or release packaging.\n\n## Required behaviour\n\nRequire a stable content-package reference and a complete provenance record\nfor every later authored content, visual, audio, localisation, map, dialogue,\nor other expressive entry. The record must identify the entry, its authoring\nsource/type, original-work assertion, any declared permitted source or licence\nreference when applicable, review status, reviewer identity/role placeholder,\nreview date placeholder, and a human creative-review disposition. Keep the\nfunctional reference boundary explicit: a compact party-based turn-adventure\nscope may inform function only; names, lore, text, art, music, maps, layouts,\nUI flows, interaction choreography, and other expression must be original.\n\nThe validator rejects a missing record, missing required fields, unmatched\ncontent reference, invalid disposition, or an entry not marked ready after\nhuman creative review. It must not infer originality from an asset filename,\nautomatically clear legal rights, or create substitute content. A reviewer may\nrecord a hold or reject disposition; only the human reviewer can record the\ncreative-review outcome. Where a declared source signals licensing or legal\nreview, retain GATE-0007 rather than allowing a technical validator to approve\nit.\n\n## Web and PWA differences\n\nThe provenance control is shared content metadata and has no separate web or\nPWA behaviour. Responsive web and installed PWA consume only content that has\npassed the same provenance validation; installation, caching, and packaging do\nnot create a provenance exception.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nDo not create or select art, audio, dialogue, maps, story, UI, fonts, icons,\nassets, licences, legal advice, automated legal clearance, release packaging,\nor target-specific content. Do not treat a reference, filename, or automated\ncheck as human creative or legal approval.\n\n## Asset assessment\n\n`asset_impact` is `not_applicable`. This is a control and review contract for\nfuture asset-required work; it creates no product asset. Do not create or\nreserve visual, source, audio, icon, illustration, font, texture, sprite, map,\nor other incidental asset; `asset_ids` and audio IDs remain blank.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Run validator fixtures proving missing provenance, required-field omissions,\n  unmatched IDs, invalid review dispositions, and unreviewed entries are\n  rejected before content admission.\n- Run a valid synthetic record fixture with an explicit original-work assertion\n  and recorded human creative-review disposition; it must be traceable to its\n  content-package reference.\n- Review the checklist against the originality boundary and prove that it\n  retains functional-only reference use and records a hold for any applicable\n  GATE-0007 legal/licensing concern.\n- Before recording the B0005 checkpoint, load this runbook and compare its\n  metadata, provenance, dependencies, requirement IDs, and ownership to the\n  manifest. Stop on a mismatch; retain R0011 and never renumber or replace it.\n\n## Evidence\n\nRetain rejected-fixture results, the valid synthetic record and reviewer\ndisposition, the originality-boundary checklist result, any GATE-0007 hold,\nand the successful loader and manifest-comparison record.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Missing or invalid provenance records prevent later authored content from\n  entering a build; validated records remain traceable to their package entry.\n- A human creative review disposition is recorded for each authored package;\n  automated validation does not claim creative, legal, or licensing approval.\n- The original-expression boundary and any applicable GATE-0007 review remain\n  explicit, with no asset, audio, or copied expression introduced.\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,45p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md; sed -n '124,173p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md; sed -n '212,245p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/specification/03-technical-contract.md; sed -n '1,100p' projects/mock-fantasy-quest-4/PROJECT_BRIEF.md; sed -n '1,100p' runbooks/README.md\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\n# 03 \u2014 Technical, data, trust, and quality contract\n\n**Inputs:** [`INITIAL.md`](../INITIAL.md), [product brief](01-product-brief.md),\nand [feature model](02-feature-model.md). This contract makes the P0 offline\nsingle-player release implementable without selecting a vendor or creating a\ndependency on the contradictory connected-service requests. It is a design\ncontract, not permission to collect data or operate services.\n\n## Architecture and requirement traceability\n\nThe production architecture is a static, client-side 2D web game: a shared\ndeterministic domain core, browser presentation/input adapters, versioned\nauthored content, and local durable storage. The web host may deliver a signed\nrelease bundle and service worker, but it is not a campaign authority. The\ncore must have no browser APIs, timers, network calls, or renderer dependencies.\n\n| Component / boundary | Requirement served | Contract |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Game shell and responsive renderer | J1, J3, J6; readable mobile-to-desktop play | Owns navigation, focus/visibility pause, semantic UI, visual settings, and rendering only. It requests domain commands and renders returned state/events. |\n| Input adapters | Keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad accessibility | Convert platform events into a shared, remappable `InputAction` vocabulary; unavailable hardware is non-fatal. No adapter may mutate campaign state directly. |\n| Deterministic rules core | Tutorial combat, quests, inventory, progression | Owns legal state transitions, seeded/randomness policy, combat resolution, reward ledger, and invariants. Command validation precedes every mutation. |\n| Content registry and validator | One town/dungeon, original content, localisation | Loads the active versioned content package; resolves IDs/keys and rejects invalid packages before play. It is read-only at runtime. |\n| Save/recovery repository | J5, long-session recovery | Owns atomic local snapshot writes, validation, quarantine, migration, retention, export/import when approved, and explicit deletion. |\n| PWA release manager | J7, install/offline/update safety | Owns precache manifest, cache integrity, install affordance, and safe-boundary activation. It never replaces a running release or a pending save write. |\n| Error boundary and consent-gated diagnostics | Crash/error recovery and privacy | Shows retry/title/recovery actions. Local diagnostic records are minimal and removable; no telemetry transport exists in P0. |\n\n### Dependency direction and state rules\n\n```text\nauthored content \u2500\u2500> content validator \u2500\u2500> deterministic rules core <\u2500\u2500 input actions\n                                            \u2502\nweb/PWA shell + renderer <\u2500\u2500 state/events \u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500> save/recovery repository\n        \u2502                                   \u2502\n        \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500> PWA release manager \u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500> error boundary / local diagnostics\n```\n\nOnly the rules core can produce durable campaign state. The shell owns\nephemeral UI state (open panels, selection, animation), while the repository\nowns only serialized snapshots and recovery metadata. Rendering, input,\ncaching, and diagnostics must not be required to make a legal game decision.\n\n## Data contract\n\n### Ownership, lifecycle, and retention\n\n## Persistence, migration, and failure semantics\n\nSave commit is a write-ahead replacement protocol: serialize a complete\nsnapshot \u2192 validate checksum/schema/invariants \u2192 write candidate under a new\nrevision \u2192 verify read-back \u2192 mark current \u2192 retain prior known-good snapshot.\nImplementations must use browser storage semantics that make the final pointer\nswitch atomic, or emulate it with immutable revision records and a validated\ncurrent pointer. Never clear the prior save before the new pointer is valid.\n\n| Condition | Required behaviour |\n| --- | --- |\n| Storage quota, permission, or unavailable local storage | Keep session playable; show non-blocking save failure and retry guidance; do not claim persistence succeeded. |\n| Interrupted/crashed write | Ignore incomplete candidate, retain/reload prior confirmed snapshot, and show recovery state at next launch. |\n| Malformed/checksum-invalid save | Quarantine bytes and metadata; do not overwrite; offer last valid checkpoint/new game and support/export guidance where feasible. |\n| Migration failure or unsupported version | Preserve original, fail closed into quarantine, and offer clear version/fallback choices. |\n| Bad content package/reference | Fail build/CI; if discovered at runtime, stop the affected route, retain save, and offer title/retry rather than corrupting campaign state. |\n| Cache/network refresh failure | Continue with known-good cached release; explain first-install offline limitation without pretending an uncached release is playable. |\n| Application fault/crash loop | Error boundary offers retry, title, and safe recovery; diagnostics remain local unless explicitly consented and a future transport is approved. |\n\nMigration functions are ordered, pure transformations from one explicit schema\nversion to the next, with fixtures for every supported predecessor. They must\nbe idempotent or record the source version so a retry cannot apply a migration\ntwice. A release may remove old migrations only after a separately approved\nsupport/retention decision.\n\n## Trust, privacy, and security\n\n- Treat content, imported saves, URL parameters, browser events, service-worker\n  messages, and persisted bytes as untrusted. Validate and bound sizes before\n  parsing; never execute content as code or render dialogue via unsafe HTML.\n- Serve production assets over HTTPS with a restrictive content security policy,\n  no third-party scripts by default, dependency lock/review, and build-time\n  asset integrity/hash manifest. Do not embed credentials in the client.\n- Use same-origin storage isolation; minimise persisted data to game state and\n  chosen preferences. Do not collect names, contact data, precise location,\n  advertising IDs, account identifiers, payment data, or gameplay analytics in\n  P0.\n- Diagnostics default off where consent is required and must state fields,\n  purpose, retention, deletion, and transport before collection. A local\n  diagnostic record excludes free-text dialogue and complete save contents.\n- There is no authorization role in P0 because there is no server resource or\n  identity boundary. Locally, a player controls their browser profile; this is\n  not a security guarantee against a device owner. Any server feature requires\n  explicit least-privilege roles, authenticated authorization on every request,\n  rate limits, audit events, deletion/retention policy, incident ownership, and\n  documented offline/outage behaviour before implementation.\n- No copied branding, assets, text, layouts, or distinctive interaction\n  sequences enter the content pipeline. Provenance/review is required for\n  shipped art, audio, writing, and dependencies.\n\n## Decisions deferred for approval\n\nThese are intentionally not selected by this contract: game engine/framework,\nrendering library, static host/CDN, CI provider, diagnostics vendor/transport,\nexact storage API/quota, browser/OS/device minimums, performance thresholds,\nlanguages, age/parental/regional obligations, service-worker rollout policy,\nand content editor format. Account identity, remote save replication,\nmultiplayer/player safety, live operations, analytics, purchases, and\nentitlements require separate product, security, privacy, cost, failure-mode,\nand support approval before they are allowed to alter this architecture.\n# Product brief \u2014 Mock Fantasy Quest 4\n\n**Source of truth:** [`INITIAL.md`](INITIAL.md). This normalized brief supports\ndownstream planning; it preserves source scope and records unknowns rather than\nchoosing them.\n\n## Product problem and outcome\n\nCasual adult players need a compact, approachable fantasy RPG that delivers a\ncomplete story without requiring a long commitment or dependable connection.\nThe first useful session is successful when a new player finishes a first\nturn-based battle and understands how to save and resume. The release outcome\nis an original short campaign that a player can complete through exploration,\ndialogue, quests, combat, rewards, saving, and a short ending.\n\n## Category, users, and core experience\n\n| Area | Normalized first-release statement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Category | Deliberately small, offline-capable, 2D single-player fantasy RPG. |\n| Users | Casual and adult players, including people using keyboard, mouse, touch, gamepad, captions, adjustable text, and visual settings. |\n| Campaign boundary | One town, one dungeon, and one guided, short ending; no further regions or chapters are required. |\n| Core loop | Explore, converse, accept or advance a quest, enter party-based turn-based combat, manage rewards/inventory, save, and continue. |\n| Experience direction | Original warm hand-painted fantasy, readable silhouettes, and a restrained palette. |\n| Functional reference | *Final Fantasy V* may inform only the high-level scope of a party-based turn-based adventure. |\n\n## Selected target systems\n\n| Target | First-release role | Input model | Delivery constraints | Out-of-scope boundary |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Responsive public web application | Primary browser surface for the complete game. | Keyboard, mouse, touch, and gamepad. | Responsive phone/tablet/desktop layout; browser compatibility and performance budget must be approved before implementation. | Native binaries, a separate web-only campaign, and browser-exclusive mechanics. |\n| Progressive web app (PWA) | Installable delivery of the same shared game core. | Same inputs where the browser/device supports them. | Offline assets and save-data behavior; safe update/cache refresh must preserve confirmed player data. | A PWA-exclusive campaign, mandatory continuous connection, or a different ruleset. |\n\nThe product uses a shared core with platform adaptation only where necessary.\nIt should work on widely used devices and under feasible slow-network\nconditions; exact supported browsers, device classes, storage quotas, and\nnetwork floor are open decisions.\n\n## First-release scope and measurable success measures\n\nIncluded scope: playable exploration; one town and one dungeon; narrative,\ndialogue, quests, progression and a short ending; onboarding/tutorial; at least\none tutorialized turn-based battle; inventory and rewards; save/load, resume,\ncheckpoints, data-loss recovery; settings and accessibility; audio/visual\nfeedback; content/level delivery; and crash reporting with player-facing error\nrecovery. The source also names account/identity, multiplayer/player safety,\nlive-service operations, and purchase/entitlement handling, but their release\nstatus remains contingent on the explicit conflict decision below.\n\nThe release is measurable when:\n\n- A new player can finish the tutorial battle, create a save, reload it, and\n  reach the short ending on each supported target.\n- The delivered campaign contains exactly one town, one dungeon, and one short\n  ending; added regions or chapters are not needed for release completion.\n- Both targets meet their stated input role; the PWA is installable and each\n  target has a tested offline, save, update, and error-recovery path.\n- Dialogue, inventory, quest, and progression UI are readable with approved\n  accessibility settings, and captions/visual settings are available where the\n  corresponding feedback exists.\n- An interruption, recoverable error, or safe update offers a player-facing\n  recovery path and does not silently discard the last confirmed save.\n\n## Later roadmap, non-goals, and reference boundary\n\nDeferred roadmap: live events/seasonal content, player-created/shared content\n(levels, mods, designs, stories), advanced analytics, and advanced\nmonetisation. They are excluded from first-release acceptance.\n\nNon-goals: copied branding, assets, text, layouts, characters, story/lore,\naudio, maps, or distinctive interactions; additional towns/dungeons/campaigns;\nnative apps; and platform-specific game variants. No cloud-sync, payment,\nmoderation, service-uptime, or backend commitment is implied before approval.\n\n| Reference boundary | Prohibited | Permitted |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Branding and copy | Names, logos, trademarks, marketing copy, story text, dialogue, characters, and world/lore. | A high-level description of a compact party-based turn-based adventure. |\n| Assets and presentation | Art, music, sound, maps, screen composition, visual language, and layouts. | Original art and UI that fulfil this brief\u2019s own direction. |\n| Interaction design | Reproducing distinctive combat screens, menus, flows, quests, or interaction choreography. | Independently designed mechanics and feedback appropriate to the requested scope. |\n\n## Constraints and safe assumptions\n\nExplicit constraints: web/PWA delivery, offline play, accessibility,\nlocalisation, privacy, safe save/recovery, slow-network feasibility, and\noriginal expression. Safe working assumptions: the offline core does not block\non a remote service; content/UI are original; local persistence is durable\nenough for confirmed saves where browser storage permits; connected features,\nif approved, degrade visibly; and crash diagnostics minimize personal data.\nThese are guardrails, not approval of a stack, vendor, backend, account model,\ndata-retention policy, or technical promise.\n\n## Open decisions requiring human approval\n\n1. **Offline versus connected scope:** Reconcile the offline single-player\n   premise with required account identity, multiplayer safety, live operations,\n   and purchase/entitlement handling. Classify each as release-critical,\n   optional connected enhancement, or deferred.\n2. **Account and data policy:** Decide whether accounts are absent, optional,\n   or mandatory; then define authentication, sync/conflict behavior, retention,\n   deletion, and offline/service-outage recovery.\n# Factory collection\n\nThis directory contains the runbook-generator's source collection. Read the\nrepository [README](../README.md) for the end-to-end workflow and Supervisor\nconfiguration.\n\n## Source files and roles\n\n| File | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| [F001.md](F001.md)\u2013[F016.md](F016.md) | **F-series factory runbooks.** They turn the brief into a generated project's detailed plan, complete work checklist, and B-series runbook-writing files. |\n| [TEMPLATE.md](TEMPLATE.md) | Required structure for a normal manually written runbook: goal, steps, success checks, and evidence. |\n\nThis directory contains only the reusable factory source. It never contains the\nthousands of runbooks for a particular product.\n\n| Series | Plain-English purpose | Output |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| F-series | The factory: understand the brief, identify the required domains, and design a complete delivery plan. | A workspace in `projects/<slug>/`, including its specification, work checklist, and B-series files. |\n| B-series | The runbook writers: write the next small set of implementation instructions. \u201cBounded\u201d means one B file may write no more than seven R files. | Up to seven R-series files per B task; extra B files are created when more areas need coverage. |\n| C-series | Catalogue checkpoints: validate the authoring catalogue, allocations, dependencies, and limits before the next writing wave. | No product work. C files are internal coordination files in `projects/<slug>/authoring-runbooks/`. |\n| D-series | Dispatchers: expand one eligible planning chapter and create the next bounded B-series writing wave when more work remains. | More B-series authoring files, not R files or product work. D files live beside B/C files in `projects/<slug>/authoring-runbooks/`. |\n| R-series | The product-work instructions: describe one small, ordered piece of real work. | Source changes, tests, assets, documentation, or other deliverables required by that R file. |\n\n`C` means **Catalogue checkpoint** and `D` means **Dispatcher**. They are\nfactory-internal coordination runbooks, so they are far less important to the\napplication than the F/B/R chain: they neither define the product nor build it.\nThey exist only to let a very large catalogue grow safely without duplicating,\nskipping, or overloading B-series writers. They share `authoring-runbooks/` and\nits durable state with B files because all three operate in the runbook-writing\nlayer, before R-series product work is run.\n\nThe generated project's `planning/implementation-catalogue-index.json` also\ncontains `IMP-*` **implementation catalogue records**. These are stable\nplanning/traceability entries, not runbooks and not another name for R files.\nThe normal chain is `REQ/NFR requirement \u2192 specification \u2192 IMP record \u2192 B\nwriter \u2192 R runbook`. `REQ-*` means a functional requirement (a behaviour or\ncapability); `NFR-*` means a non-functional requirement (a quality or\nconstraint such as accessibility, security, performance, or privacy). They\nlive in `specification/requirements.md`, `requirements.json`, and the\n`traceability-matrix.md` for the generated project. An IMP record may map to\none or several R files, or wait for a dependency, so `REQ-0007`, `NFR-0004`,\n`IMP-0007`, and `R0007` intentionally have independent IDs. Each generated R\nfile records the relevant requirement IDs in `requirement_ids` and the relevant\nIMP IDs in `source_catalogue_ids` front matter.\n\nEvery R-series file must make an explicit asset decision in its front matter:\n`asset_impact: required` with stable comma-separated `asset_ids`, or\n`asset_impact: not_applicable` with a blank `asset_ids`. Asset-required files\nalso describe asset purpose, variants, ownership, original-art constraints, and\nverification in an `## Asset assessment` section. This lets the Supervisor use\nits opt-in asset lane only where the product work genuinely needs it.\n\nEvery R-series file also makes an explicit audio decision: `audio_impact:\nrequired` with stable `audio_ids`, a cue brief, duration, loop policy, and\naudio-style version; or `audio_impact: not_applicable` with the documented\nempty/default values. When required, Supervisor uses the independent local\naudio lane\u2014cue director, ACE-Step 1.5 XL Turbo generator, then technical and\nprovenance QA\u2014before the coding stage. F004 creates an audio-direction and cue\nmap only when the product actually needs music, sound effects, voice, or\naudible feedback.\n\nEvery R-series file must also carry traceability and provenance metadata:\n`requirement_ids` identifies the `REQ-*` and `NFR-*` requirements it fulfils or\nverifies; `source_specifications` links to the smallest relevant canonical specification sections,\n`source_catalogue_ids` identifies its implementation-catalogue records,\n`authoring_batch` names its B-series writer, and `factory_stages` records the\nF-series stages that established the requirement. This lets an implementation\nagent retrieve focused canonical context without treating procedural F/B files\nas its requirements source.\n\nFor example, a game may eventually have R runbooks for story, combat, content,\nsaves, and platform delivery; an IDE may have R runbooks for language services,\nediting, debugging, and extensions. The F-series derives those areas from the\nbrief rather than assuming either example.\n\n`F001`\u2013`F004` understand the brief and domains, `F005`\u2013`F010` make the delivery\nmap, `F011`\u2013`F013` create and check the specification and work checklist, and\n`F014` creates the first B-series runbook-writing files. When Supervisor runs\nthose B files, each one writes up to seven R-series product-work runbooks. If\nmore R files are required, B dispatcher files create more B files. `F015`\u2013`F016`\nestablish quality and handoff material.\n\n## Run the collection\n\nFrom the repository root, create a named project brief with `supervisor initial`,\nreview `projects/<project-name>/INITIAL.md`, then run:\n\n```zsh\nsupervisor-run --project <project-name>\n```\n\n`--run-initial` runs only `F001`, which is useful for checking that the named\nbrief can be normalized before committing to the full factory. `--project`\nreads that workspace's `INITIAL.md`, runs every F-series factory stage, then\ndynamically follows its registered B-series and R-series collections. It keeps\nstate inside the named workspace and stops only for a real failure or explicit\nreview gate.\n\nGenerated runbook state is kept in the named workspace's `.state/` directory,\n\nexec\n/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n -i 'runbook.*(load|validat)|front.?matter|required sections?|source_catalogue_ids' supervisor scripts -g '*.py' -g '*.md' | sed -n '1,220p'\" in /Users/simon/Dev/github/jakowicz/runbook-generator\n succeeded in 0ms:\nsupervisor/supervisor/manage.py:23:from .runbooks import load_task\nsupervisor/supervisor/cli.py:23:from .runbooks import load_task\nsupervisor/supervisor/cli.py:60:    parser.add_argument(\"--task-id\", help=\"Runbook task ID, for example D005. Automatically loads runbooks/<ID>.md when present.\")\nsupervisor/supervisor/cli.py:691:                store.reopen_task(runbook.stem, \"Authoring output validation failed: \" + \"; \".join(errors))\nsupervisor/supervisor/runbooks.py:42:        provenance_metadata = {\"source_specifications\", \"source_catalogue_ids\", \"authoring_batch\", \"factory_stages\"}\nsupervisor/supervisor/runbooks.py:50:        source_catalogue_ids = [value.strip() for value in metadata[\"source_catalogue_ids\"].split(\",\") if value.strip()]\nsupervisor/supervisor/runbooks.py:52:        if not source_specifications or not source_catalogue_ids or not factory_stages:\nsupervisor/supervisor/runbooks.py:130:        source_catalogue_ids=[value.strip() for value in metadata.get(\"source_catalogue_ids\", \"\").split(\",\") if value.strip()],\nsupervisor/supervisor/models.py:100:    source_catalogue_ids: list[str] = Field(default_factory=list)\nsupervisor/README.md:320:    start[\"Run a task, runbook, or task range\"] --> load[\"Load configuration, runbook, SQLite history, and durable checkpoint\"]\nsupervisor/README.md:440:`../.